---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="kernic for AI agents"
  description="Give your coding agent a design system instead of letting it invent colors."
  active="agents"
>
  <h1>kernic for AI agents</h1>
  <p>
    Apps built by AI often look the same because the agent invents colors as it goes.
    Give it your system instead. One command and one rule, and everything it writes uses
    your tokens.
  </p>

  <h2>The one-command setup</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="shell" code={`kernic context midnight-neon -o .   # writes design.md + tokens.json`} />
  <p>
    <code>design.md</code> includes usage rules plus every token. The adjacent
    <a href="/docs/tokens">tokens.json</a> is W3C DTCG format, so Figma, Terrazzo, and Style
    Dictionary read it too.
  </p>

  <h2>Point the agent at it</h2>
  <p>One line in <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>.cursorrules</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="markdown"
    code={`Visual design: follow ./design.md exactly.\nUse only its tokens. Never invent raw hex values.`}
  />

  <h2>Or go live over MCP</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="shell" code={`claude mcp add kernic -- npx kernic mcp`} />
  <ul>
    <li><code>list_systems</code>: discover saved systems</li>
    <li><code>get_system</code>: full spec of one system</li>
    <li><code>get_tokens</code>: tokens in design-md, css, tailwind, fonts, dtcg or json format</li>
    <li><code>list_vibes</code>: available vibe presets</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="docs-callout">
    Proof it works: you're looking at it. This site was built by an agent working strictly from
    a <code>kernic context</code> export.
  </div>

  <h2>Why it holds up</h2>
  <p>
    Token files include a version number and an extensions block. New features (motion, shadows,
    multi-brand) land without breaking existing files. Your systems are local JSON. They stay local.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
